1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,176
Aarav plugs his phone
charger into the wall socket.

2
00:00:03,526 --> 00:00:06,233
The socket gives
alternating current, or AC.

3
00:00:06,233 --> 00:00:08,666
It flips direction fifty times a second.

4
00:00:09,016 --> 00:00:11,927
But a phone battery needs
direct current, or DC.

5
00:00:11,927 --> 00:00:12,883
One way only.

6
00:00:13,233 --> 00:00:15,332
So how does the charger turn AC into DC?

7
00:00:15,932 --> 00:00:19,240
Start with a water pipe that
has a one-way flap inside.

8
00:00:19,590 --> 00:00:22,934
Push the water forward,
and the flap swings open.

9
00:00:22,934 --> 00:00:23,905
Water flows.

10
00:00:24,255 --> 00:00:26,979
Push it backward, and the flap slams shut.

11
00:00:26,979 --> 00:00:27,974
Nothing flows.

12
00:00:28,574 --> 00:00:32,375
A diode is that one-way flap,
but for electric current.

13
00:00:32,725 --> 00:00:36,254
The arrow in its symbol shows
the only way current can go.

14
00:00:36,604 --> 00:00:40,630
When the diode lets current through,
we say it is forward biased.

15
00:00:40,980 --> 00:00:43,927
When it blocks current, we
say it is reverse biased.

16
00:00:44,527 --> 00:00:48,061
Changing AC into one-way
current is called rectification.

17
00:00:48,411 --> 00:00:51,333
The circuit that does it
is called a rectifier.

18
00:00:51,983 --> 00:00:54,892
Let's build the simplest
one on the breadboard.

19
00:00:55,242 --> 00:00:59,197
An AC supply, one diode,
and a load resistor, RL.

20
00:00:59,547 --> 00:01:01,886
The load is the thing we want to power.

21
00:01:02,236 --> 00:01:04,315
Watch the positive half cycle.

22
00:01:04,315 --> 00:01:06,292
The diode is forward biased.

23
00:01:06,642 --> 00:01:09,103
Electrons flow, and the
output copies the input hump.

24
00:01:09,453 --> 00:01:11,470
Now the negative half cycle.

25
00:01:11,470 --> 00:01:13,486
The diode is reverse biased.

26
00:01:13,836 --> 00:01:15,355
It blocks the current.

27
00:01:15,355 --> 00:01:17,076
The output stays at zero.

28
00:01:17,426 --> 00:01:21,184
So the output is a row of
humps, with gaps in between.

29
00:01:21,534 --> 00:01:23,201
It never goes negative.

30
00:01:23,201 --> 00:01:25,610
It flows one way, but it pulses.

31
00:01:25,960 --> 00:01:27,368
We call it pulsating DC.

32
00:01:27,718 --> 00:01:30,064
Only half of each wave gets through.

33
00:01:30,064 --> 00:01:32,163
So this is a half-wave rectifier.

34
00:01:32,813 --> 00:01:34,577
A diode is a one-way gate.

35
00:01:34,577 --> 00:01:36,873
It lets only half the wave through.

36
00:01:37,673 --> 00:01:39,557
So how much DC do we really get?

37
00:01:39,557 --> 00:01:40,641
Let's work it out.

38
00:01:40,991 --> 00:01:43,071
Call the peak of the input V m.

39
00:01:43,771 --> 00:01:47,163
In the positive half, the
output follows the input.

40
00:01:47,163 --> 00:01:48,495
Its peak is V m.

41
00:01:49,195 --> 00:01:51,708
In the negative half, the output is zero.

42
00:01:52,408 --> 00:01:55,854
Squash one hump flat, like
levelling sand in a box.

43
00:01:56,204 --> 00:01:59,083
Its average height is two V m over pi.

44
00:01:59,783 --> 00:02:02,241
But the humps fill only half the time.

45
00:02:02,241 --> 00:02:03,204
So halve it.

46
00:02:03,904 --> 00:02:06,053
V d c equals V m over pi.

47
00:02:06,053 --> 00:02:08,855
About zero point three one eight V m.

48
00:02:09,555 --> 00:02:11,727
Current follows the same rule.

49
00:02:11,727 --> 00:02:13,826
I d c equals I m over pi.

50
00:02:14,476 --> 00:02:16,997
Now three numbers every exam loves.

51
00:02:17,347 --> 00:02:22,068
In the negative half, the blocked
diode faces the full peak, backwards.

52
00:02:22,418 --> 00:02:24,669
That is the peak inverse voltage.

53
00:02:24,669 --> 00:02:26,313
Here, it equals V m.

54
00:02:26,913 --> 00:02:29,500
One hump comes out for each input cycle.

55
00:02:29,850 --> 00:02:33,425
So the output frequency
equals the input frequency.

56
00:02:34,025 --> 00:02:37,702
At best, forty point six percent of
the input power becomes DC power.

57
00:02:38,352 --> 00:02:39,466
Worked example.

58
00:02:39,466 --> 00:02:43,106
The input has a peak of
twenty volts, at fifty hertz.

59
00:02:43,456 --> 00:02:47,094
The load is one kilo-ohm,
and the diode is ideal.

60
00:02:47,444 --> 00:02:49,777
V d c equals twenty over pi.

61
00:02:49,777 --> 00:02:52,381
About six point three seven volts.

62
00:02:52,981 --> 00:02:57,374
I d c is six point three seven
volts over one thousand ohms.

63
00:02:57,974 --> 00:03:00,079
That is about six point
three seven milliamperes.

64
00:03:00,679 --> 00:03:04,865
The diode must survive a peak
inverse voltage of twenty volts.

65
00:03:05,465 --> 00:03:08,278
And the output humps arrive
fifty times a second.

66
00:03:08,928 --> 00:03:10,389
Now, the exam trap.

67
00:03:10,739 --> 00:03:15,282
Many students say a half-wave output
has a frequency of one hundred hertz.

68
00:03:15,632 --> 00:03:16,571
It does not.

69
00:03:16,571 --> 00:03:18,212
One hump per input cycle.

70
00:03:18,212 --> 00:03:20,317
Fifty hertz in, fifty hertz out.

71
00:03:20,967 --> 00:03:22,117
Where is this asked?

72
00:03:22,117 --> 00:03:23,862
PUC, CBSE and ISC board papers.

73
00:03:24,212 --> 00:03:28,095
NEET twenty twenty two asked
exactly this frequency question.

74
00:03:28,445 --> 00:03:32,028
Expect to draw the circuit and
waveforms, or find the DC value.

75
00:03:32,678 --> 00:03:33,578
Try this.

76
00:03:33,523 --> 00:03:37,657
Thirty sine one hundred pi t
volts feeds a half-wave rectifier.

77
00:03:38,007 --> 00:03:39,789
The load is two kilo-ohms.

78
00:03:39,789 --> 00:03:42,790
Find the DC load current
and output frequency.

79
00:03:48,390 --> 00:03:49,290
B.

80
00:03:48,748 --> 00:03:51,746
Four point seven seven
milliamperes, at fifty hertz.

81
00:03:52,096 --> 00:03:54,050
The peak is thirty volts.

82
00:03:54,050 --> 00:03:56,309
So V d c is thirty over pi.

83
00:03:56,659 --> 00:03:59,278
That is about nine point five five volts.

84
00:03:59,628 --> 00:04:01,275
Divide by two thousand ohms.

85
00:04:01,275 --> 00:04:03,826
That gives four point
seven seven milliamperes.

86
00:04:04,176 --> 00:04:07,939
Omega is one hundred
pi, so f is fifty hertz.

87
00:04:07,939 --> 00:04:09,620
The frequency stays.

88
00:04:10,270 --> 00:04:11,817
Screenshot this card.

89
00:04:15,017 --> 00:04:17,054
Next, full-wave and bridge rectifiers.

90
00:04:17,054 --> 00:04:19,246
How do we use both halves of the wave?
